declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 9, $j$3.1 Paul the Letter Writer and Romans: Sin and Grace$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Romans 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Romans/3"}, {"title": "Romans 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Romans/5"}, {"title": "Romans 6 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Romans/6"}], "body": "Read Romans 3, Romans 5 and Romans 6 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThirteen of the twenty seven books of the New Testament are letters written by the apostle Paul. These letters, called epistles, were sent to churches and to friends to teach, correct and encourage. They were written to real people facing real problems, and they are arranged in the Bible by length, not by date. The longest and most important is Romans, written to the church in Rome. Romans explains the gospel. All people have sinned, no one can earn salvation by good works, and God gives righteousness freely by grace through faith in Jesus Christ. The wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life.\n\nKey words: epistle, sin, grace, justified, faith.\n\nYour task: (1) Explain what an epistle is and who wrote thirteen of them. (2) Write Romans 3:23 in your own words. (3) Tell the difference between the wages of sin and the gift of God in Romans 6:23.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Welcome to Unit 3. The Gospels told us what Jesus did, and Acts told us how the church began. Now we study the letters. A letter in the New Testament is called an epistle, and the epistles explain what the good news means for daily life.\n\nThirteen of these letters were written by the apostle Paul, the man who was once Saul the persecutor. Paul wrote to churches he had started and to friends like Timothy, and he wrote about real problems, such as division, false teaching and fear. The letters are arranged in the Bible from the longest to the shortest, not by the date they were written. The longest is Romans, and it is the most complete explanation of the gospel in the Bible.\n\nPaul wrote Romans to the believers in Rome, a city he had not yet visited. He began by saying in Romans 1:16, I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ: for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth. Then he gave the bad news first. Both Jews and Gentiles have a problem. Romans 3:23 says, For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God. Nobody can reach God by their own goodness.\n\nThen comes the good news. Romans 3:24 says, Being justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. To be justified means to be declared right with God, as if we had never sinned. It is a free gift that we receive by faith. Paul pointed to the greatest proof of God's love in Romans 5:8, But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.\n\nPaul also made clear that grace is never an excuse to keep on sinning. In Romans 6:23 he wrote, For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord. Wages are what you earn, and a gift is something you cannot earn. Sin pays death, and God offers life as a gift.\n\nYour task is to explain what an epistle is, write Romans 3:23 in your own words, and tell the difference between wages and a gift. Then take the four question check.\n\nSalvation is a gift that we receive, never a wage that we earn. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.1 Paul the Letter Writer and Romans: Sin and Grace$j$);
end
$seed$;
